Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- Conductor cross-section plus number of cores, both of which change the accessory kit.
- Measured cable insulation diameter, because the same conductor size exists with different wall thicknesses.
- Screen or armour construction, which decides whether an earth continuity path is part of the kit.
- Lug type and stud size where the accessory lands on a terminal rather than inside a joint.
- Service environment: indoor, outdoor, buried, duct or cable tray.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- EN 50393, test requirements for accessories for LV distribution cables up to 0.6/1 kV.
- IEC 60502-1, the cable standard that fixes the insulation geometry the accessory has to fit.
- IEC 60684 series, flexible insulating sleeving base material requirements.
- UL 94, flame behaviour of the polymer parts.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Unwind the supporting core completely before releasing the sleeve. A partly unwound core produces a local flat spot and an uneven recovered wall.
- Clean and dry the cable insulation before the sleeve is positioned. A cold shrink interface does not tolerate dust or moisture trapped under it.
- Centre the sleeve on the joint before the last turn of the core is released, since elastic recovery cannot be repositioned afterwards.
- Store cores undeformed, out of direct sunlight, and within the storage temperature range on the datasheet.
- Do not use a knife near the recovered wall. Score marks on the outer surface are a tracking path in damp service.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Water ingress along the lug or core interface | Incomplete sealing at the end of the sleeve or a mastic strip omitted | Fit mastic strips to drawing and respect the stated overlap onto the cable sheath. |
| Uneven recovery, local flat spot | Supporting core only partly unwound, or sleeve stored deformed | Unwind the full core, store cores unloaded, reject sleeves with visible deformation. |
| Loose fit after installation | Accessory selected on conductor size while the actual insulation OD is outside the range | Measure insulation OD and select from the accessory size table. |
| Surface tracking in outdoor cabinets | Contamination and moisture on a damaged outer wall | Handle without scoring the wall and keep the specified creepage on the terminal side. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Low voltage distribution joints and terminations in utility and industrial networks.
- Cable accessory OEMs and cable manufacturers building kits for export markets.
- Electrical distributors supporting field repair and maintenance crews.
- Industrial EPC work where a hot work permit is not available for a joint or termination.

